    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0230",
      "sequence": 242,
      "meaning": "Hearing the sound of bracelets, girdle-bells and anklets, Rāma reflected inwardly and told Lakṣmaṇa, ‘It is as though Love has sounded his kettledrum, resolved in his heart to conquer the world.’ Saying this, he turned and looked that way; his eyes became partridges fixed on the moon of Sītā’s face. His beautiful eyes grew motionless, as though Nimi had bashfully abandoned the domain of sight. Seeing Sītā’s beauty he found delight, praising it inwardly but unable to speak: it was as though Brahmā had fashioned the universe with all his skill and manifested it for display; she made beauty itself beautiful, like a flame kindled in the house of splendor. The poets have soiled every simile through prior use—what could he compare with Videha’s daughter? Describing Sītā’s beauty within his heart and considering his own state, the pure-minded Lord spoke to his younger brother in words suited to the moment.",
